zens movement is ready to be barred  I think it's latent I think it could be  brought to life so that is the first  area I think the secondaries is this  away from consumerism and individualism  and the domination of nature and war  towards questions of the quality of life  and the ecological sensibility and human  solidarity that would also be the second  pillar that would signal that a shift  towards a great transition is possible  [Music]  I think if we get to a this livable  future it will be because Humanity has  found a way to balance the paradigms of  global responsibilities with diversity  and pluralism in modes of culture and  development when we take our trips to  outer space and we look back we see an  integral blue planet you don't see  imaginary lines of states and so that  this globalism the sense of the earth as  a natural unit for a human organization  and identity in some ways more profound  than are the imaginary boundaries of of  nation-state when we look at the  question of value change that could  undergird this great transition I think  we have to look at the process of change  in in two arenas one in affluent  countries and one in developing  countries and affluent countries we're  already seeing the precursors the  questioning of the meaning of the good  life turning towards questions about the  quality of existence not just the  quantity of things as defining success  and well-being so that's one area is a  value change is in terms of what it is  to be a whole person in a fulfilled  person I think when we come to the  question of the developing world there's  an even more profound transition that we  can imagine to begin to define their own  agenda for development that there are  plural paths to modernity not a single  path and that their own paths can  leapfrog a lot of the mistakes that have  gone on in the industrialized world and  begin to come up with forms and models  of development that  build on local traditions and customs  and culture another area comes to the  question of the environment people I  think are coming to see that the  rapaciousness of the past must give way  to a sense of humanity's place in the  web of life and dependence on its health  sustainability and bounty so this is a  major almost Copernican shift in  awareness Copernicus taught us that we  didn't live at the center of the  universe but that we were on a planet  that revolved around the Sun well this  new Copernican revolution I think is  telling us that we are not at the center  of Earth we are part of the web of life  of this grand ecology and if we don't  take care to see our place within it  then we may go down with it  [Music]  I think there are three major areas that  we need to move down to begin this  transition and those areas I think are  understanding vision and action I think  we need to gain a better grasp of this  world we live in in this emergent global  system both as scientists and as  citizens we need to struggle to expand  our mental maps to traverse over space  to take into account a global panorama  the span over time to think over many  decades where people from all over the  world can find their place within the  broad umbrella that unifies their  various concerns into a broad-based  process of awareness building and  political change that can actually begin  to steer things in a fundamentally  different direction so when we do reach  our Time of Troubles which well could  come a time of systemic global crises  that there is the political space and  possibility for recovery more than  recovery for building and beginning a  new history  [Music]  you  [Music]

